    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on some walnut cabinet joinery right now and I'm running into a frustrating issue. Every time I try to cut the dovetails, the wood fibers are splintering at the tips of the pins, even though I'm being careful with the layout. It's compromising the fit and making the joints look messy. Can you give me some advice on how to manage the grain direction better or what I should check with my chisel sharpness?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When cutting dovetails, ensure that the blade angle is correct, typically around 90 degrees, for clean cuts through the wood fibers. Keep your chisels sharp, as dull blades can cause tearing. Also, make sure the wood fibers are running in an even direction on both sides of the joint to minimize splintering. Use a high-quality sharp chisel, apply gentle, consistent pressure, and chisel away slowly to prevent creating tear-out on your dovetail joints. Check out a video on dovetail chiseling for visual guidance on proper technique.",
