    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the tip. i've definitely checked the sharpness and they're razor keen, so i don't think it's the tool itself. i'm also being really careful with the pressure. it's almost like the wood is just resisting the cut or something, like the grain is more irregular than it looks on the surface. is there anything specific i should look for in the wood grain before I start my layout to avoid this?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Inspect the walnut under good lighting and look for any subtle irregularities or knots that may skew the grain pattern. Even seemingly straight grain patterns have micro-variations, which can increase splintering. Use a combination of visual inspection and running your hands gently over the surface to feel for inconsistencies. Be aware of any burls or reaction wood, which may be denser and tougher, causing unexpected resistance. Consider adjusting your joinery approach to accommodate potential grain irregularities, such as choosing a less aggressive angle or slower chiseling pace. If possible, examine more stock from the same piece to anticipate these variations before carving out your final set of dovetails. Additionally, some woodworkers use sharp tongue depressors or metal jigs in the initial cuts to help control the tear-out before they fully commit to precise cuts.",
      "source": "target",
