    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i'll try checking for reaction wood or burls specifically. it's just weird because this walnut was supposed to be pretty high grade and consistent. do you think moisture content could be playing a role in why the grain feels so unpredictable or \"tough\" in certain spots? like, could it be drying unevenly and changing the way the fibers respond to the chisel?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, moisture content can significantly impact the wood's behavior during joinery. Walnut, like other hardwoods, can vary in moisture content both within a single log and among pieces from the same log due to uneven drying. It's possible that areas of the wood that felt tough may have had decreased moisture content, which can make them denser and more challenging to cut without causing splintering. The optimal moisture content for most advance hardwood joinery is approximately 6-8%. If you haven't checked the moisture content recently, it might be worth using a moisture meter and comparing readings across various sections of your piece to ensure the wood is properly seasoned. Additionally, utilizing consistent and ample humidity in your work area can help maintain wood stability during the joinery process. If you've already seasoned the wood, consider re-examining other factors like the cut depth above or below the median growth ring, as this also contributes to the cutting resistance.",
      "source": "target",
